WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.847
Web application security protects data, identity, workflows, and trust boundaries.

2
00:00:04.847 --> 00:00:10.662
It is broader than network controls and narrower than vague best practices.

3
00:00:10.662 --> 00:00:30.533
It is the practical engineering work of making sure users are correctly identified, actions are properly authorized, input is handled safely, output is rendered in the right context, secrets are protected, dependencies are managed, and configuration supports the intended security model.

4
00:00:30.533 --> 00:00:38.288
Strong programs combine secure design, validation, access control, safe coding, configuration hygiene, testing, monitoring, and remediation.

5
00:00:38.288 --> 00:00:47.496
Attack surface mapping helps teams see where users, APIs, roles, files, third-party systems, client-side code, and deployment settings interact.

6
00:00:47.496 --> 00:00:53.312
Data flow and trust boundary analysis help teams understand where assumptions change.

7
00:00:53.312 --> 00:01:01.551
Authentication, sessions, authorization, injection defenses, dependency management, and error handling all work together rather than standing alone.

8
00:01:01.551 --> 00:01:07.852
The goal is reliable, secure application behavior under normal, unexpected, and hostile conditions.

9
00:01:07.852 --> 00:01:23.845
A secure web application should continue to enforce the right rules when input is malformed, when users take unexpected paths, when integrations fail, when dependencies age, and when attackers probe for weak assumptions.

10
00:01:23.845 --> 00:01:28.207
Security is not a single scan or final gate.

11
00:01:28.207 --> 00:01:34.992
It is a recurring engineering discipline that follows the application from design through operation.
